## AI Agent Reasoning

### Context
IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md item 7 (HIGH) flagged that arrow-key navigation
across the habit table grid was unimplemented, and that keyboard focus
movement needed explicit test coverage. While implementing, also found
a real bug: HabitCell's aria-label used the icon id instead of the
habit name, contradicting the spec's
'Habit: <name> — <date> — marked/unmarked' format.

### Decisions Made
- Roving tabindex pattern (one tabIndex=0 cell, rest -1) chosen over
  giving every cell tabIndex=0: matches the standard ARIA grid keyboard
  pattern and keeps Tab-key traversal of the page to one stop per grid
  instead of one stop per cell (habits x days could be 30+ cells/row).
- Kept native <table>/<td> semantics rather than switching to
  role=grid/row/gridcell: the existing table already conveys row/column
  relationships via built-in table semantics, and screen readers handle
  a real <table> combined with roving tabindex + aria-pressed buttons
  correctly, so no role rewrite was needed for this increment.
- Single source of focus truth (activeCell state + cellRefs matrix) in
  HabitTable rather than local state per HabitCell, so Up/Down/Left/
  Right can move across sibling cells that don't otherwise share state.
- onFocusCell updates activeCell whenever any cell gains focus (not
  just via arrow keys) so plain Tab navigation also keeps the roving
  tabindex correct.

### Trade-offs
- Did not implement Shift+Arrow range selection or multi-cell
  bulk-marking here — that is explicitly item 8 in the plan (bulk
  marking / undo-redo) and is a separate, larger increment.
- Used fireEvent.keyDown + explicit act() wrapping in the vitest test
  instead of @testing-library/user-event, since user-event isn't a
  project dependency and adding it was out of scope for this fix.
